FATAL_OBJECTIONS:
  - systems_reviewer cites pandas issues #20642, #43929, #48757 — three independent prior reports of the EXACT phenomenon (float16 Series.mean() → inf/NaN from accumulator overflow). PR #64791 is a MERGED fix upcasting float16 in nanmean/nansum for pandas 3.1. The packet's claim "no GitHub issue for 'pandas float16 Series.mean overflow inf/nan' on current versions" is demonstrably false. This is not near-prior-art; it IS the same bug, reported, triaged, and patched by the framework maintainers.
  - The "neither endpoint predicts it" leg of stress-test A fails: numpy's official documentation EXPLICITLY warns that float16 reductions are precision-limited and recommends passing dtype=float64 (novelty_killer cites numpy.mean reference docs). One endpoint does predict the hazard from its own side.
  - The "silent" framing is challenged by evaluation_prosecutor: inf/NaN are IEEE-754 poison pills that trigger immediate ValueError in sklearn (check_array), making the failure LOUD in any standard downstream pipeline, not silent. No killer experiment was run to demonstrate silent model corruption.
REQUIRED_EVIDENCE:
  - Exhaustive pandas issue-tracker search acknowledging #20642, #43929, #48757, PR #64791, and explaining what residual contribution survives after these.
  - Downstream pipeline survival-rate test: does pandas inf/NaN actually propagate silently through sklearn/model training, or does it crash immediately at validation?
  - Prevalence data for fp16 tabular feature storage + pandas reduction in real production systems (GitHub/Kaggle/HF dataset dtype audits, not just one California housing cast).
  - Reproduction on pandas 2.2.x (actual widely-deployed version) AND post-fix pandas 3.1+ to establish whether the seam still exists on versions people use.
MANDATORY_BASELINES:
  - pandas issues #20642, #43929, #48757 and PR #64791 as direct prior art (authors must differentiate or concede)
  - numpy.mean official dtype promotion documentation (float16 → float32 accumulator is documented, not discovered)
  - sklearn.utils.check_array NaN/Inf rejection as downstream loud-failure baseline
PRIOR_ART_ADEQUATE: no
