RATIONALE: The systems_reviewer's RED is backed by hard, verifiable evidence — three pandas issues spanning 2018–2022 reporting the identical float16 mean→inf/NaN overflow, plus a merged PR fixing it. This RED is unrebutted by any other reviewer or by the packet. Per committee rules, I cannot emit GREEN with an unrebutted RED or inadequate prior-art search, and both conditions hold here.

The four YELLOWs converge from independent angles on the same conclusion: (1) novelty_killer — mechanism is textbook IEEE 754 overflow, per-tool behaviors are individually documented, numpy docs explicitly warn about float16 reductions; (2) evaluation_prosecutor — "silent" framing collapses if downstream sklearn validation catches inf/NaN immediately (no killer experiment run to disprove this); (3) theory_skeptic — the entire divergence reduces to one inequality (running_sum > 65504), fully predictable from fp16 spec × each tool's documented accumulator dtype, making the "coupling" a Cartesian product of known policies, not an emergent phenomenon; (4) product_realist — fp16 tabular feature storage is uncommon, workaround is trivial (astype float64), no prevalence data provided.

Critically, this claim fails BOTH stress tests flagged by the orchestrator: (A) the "neither endpoint predicts" argument is false — numpy's own docs warn, and the data side can compute mean×N>65504 from the fp16 spec; (B) no evidence that fp16 feature storage + pandas reduction is a realistic deployed pattern. The claim also fails the CLAIM-0059 comparison: NFKC-vs-NFC produced a nonlinear statistical surprise (95% dup-escape) that neither side's documentation could derive; here the effect is a one-line inequality applied to a known accumulator policy. The honest altitude is "well-executed reproduction of a known, already-patched pandas bug with a useful cross-tool comparison table" — a documentation/blog-post contribution, not a publishable new cross-area coupling. Converge.
